{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model assumes a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slightly upward) regardless of recent price spikes, failing to capture the high-amplitude oscillations (e.g., the jump to 73.14 and subsequent drop to 64.92) seen in the true data.",
    "Apply aggressive mean reversion after upward spikes: When the true price exceeds the model's forecast by more than 2-3 EUR (e.g., day 3-4 of the horizon), the model fails to predict the immediate correction; forecasts must be adjusted downward by 3-5 EUR following any breakout above 71 EUR.",
    "Ignore sentiment signals as leading indicators: The sentiment history shows sporadic positive spikes (0.33) that do not correlate with the model's failure to predict the subsequent price surge; sentiment should not be used to justify the model's static upward drift.",
    "Reduce long-horizon bias: The model exhibits a persistent upward bias in the 15-30 day horizon, predicting prices near 72-73 EUR while the true outcome reverts to the 65-67 EUR range; forecasts beyond day 10 should be capped at the recent historical mean (approx. 68-69 EUR) rather than extrapolated upward.",
    "Account for lag in trend reversal: The model reacts too slowly to the peak at 73.14, continuing to forecast increases for several days after the peak; correction rules must force a downward slope in the forecast immediately upon detecting a local maximum in the true price series."
  ]
}
